(He plays a sentimental track.)

259
00:09:49,683 --> 00:09:50,783
All right.

260
00:09:50,783 --> 00:09:52,183
(He opens the small window.)

261
00:09:52,913 --> 00:09:55,723
So that's the room where you can ventilate...

262
00:09:55,723 --> 00:09:57,293
- although the window is tiny. - Right.

263
00:09:57,553 --> 00:09:59,193
You can't grill mackerels in your house.

264
00:10:00,223 --> 00:10:01,963
- It could kill you. - The moment he grills it,

265
00:10:01,963 --> 00:10:03,063
he'll pass out right away.

266
00:10:03,223 --> 00:10:04,363
- He'll cough like this. - If he boils...

267
00:10:04,363 --> 00:10:06,393
I bet the smell will last for six months.

268
00:10:06,593 --> 00:10:08,103
If he boils snow crabs,

269
00:10:08,103 --> 00:10:10,503
the smell will last for eight months in my opinion.

270
00:10:11,733 --> 00:10:14,403
(He's enjoying the music, not boiling snow crabs.)

271
00:10:15,643 --> 00:10:18,673
When I want to get sentimental with some music,

272
00:10:19,013 --> 00:10:20,983
I use the music room.

273
00:10:21,613 --> 00:10:23,583
It's the only well-lit room...

274
00:10:23,583 --> 00:10:26,053
where I can enjoy a cup of coffee.

275
00:10:26,053 --> 00:10:29,453
So when I listen to music, I usually go to that room.

276
00:10:29,453 --> 00:10:30,993
(The music room is a great place for some sunlight.)

277
00:10:31,193 --> 00:10:32,253
It looks like a prison cell.

278
00:10:32,693 --> 00:10:33,893
- That's... - They look like prison bars.

279
00:10:33,893 --> 00:10:34,993
They turned into prison bars.

280
00:10:35,293 --> 00:10:38,163
So you can only enjoy some sunlight during your free time?

281
00:10:38,163 --> 00:10:39,963
- Only model prisoners can. - Model prisoners.

282
00:10:40,333 --> 00:10:42,703
- If he misbehaves, he can't go out. - He must behave then.

283
00:10:42,863 --> 00:10:44,273
He has the face of a model prisoner.

284
00:10:44,273 --> 00:10:45,473
The face of a model prisoner?

285
00:10:46,833 --> 00:10:48,973
(Model Prisoner Seong Jae gets to enjoy some sunlight with music.)

286
00:10:48,973 --> 00:10:50,413
(Focused)

287
00:10:51,743 --> 00:10:54,843
(He listens to the music intently...)

288
00:10:54,843 --> 00:10:57,183
(while his eyes are glued to his phone.)

289
00:10:57,983 --> 00:11:00,353
(He enjoys his lazy afternoon while listening to music.)

290
00:11:02,553 --> 00:11:04,053
(He gets up at the sound of the alarm.)

291
00:11:04,923 --> 00:11:06,793
(He approaches the door.)

292
00:11:06,793 --> 00:11:07,993
- Even his door is black. - His door is black too.

293
00:11:07,993 --> 00:11:09,063
That's crazy.

294
00:11:11,833 --> 00:11:13,093
He must've gotten food delivered.

295
00:11:13,363 --> 00:11:14,463
Is it jjajangmyeon?

296
00:11:18,603 --> 00:11:19,833
(Flicking off the light)

297
00:11:21,473 --> 00:11:25,113
You should have made a request to deliver the food in a black bag.

298
00:11:26,413 --> 00:11:28,113
(My plastic bag should be black too.)

299
00:11:28,113 --> 00:11:29,183
Right on.

300
00:11:29,183 --> 00:11:31,183
- "I'm sorry, but..." - And the rice should be black rice.

301
00:11:31,183 --> 00:11:33,923
- People don't use black bags now. - "give me a black plastic bag."

302
00:11:33,923 --> 00:11:34,983
Right.

303
00:11:36,723 --> 00:11:39,023
(Delivering Wilson back to the dark living room)

304
00:11:39,623 --> 00:11:41,223
Watch me eat.

305
00:11:43,363 --> 00:11:45,293
(Safely placing it back in the corner)

306
00:11:47,263 --> 00:11:49,833
(Let me sit down too.)

307
00:11:51,873 --> 00:11:53,633
- You eat in the dark like that? - What?

308
00:11:53,973 --> 00:11:56,373
- You must buy night-vision goggles. - Right.

309
00:11:57,643 --> 00:11:59,243
(Opening the package in the dark)

310
00:11:59,513 --> 00:12:01,143
(Eyes fixed on the food)

311
00:12:01,143 --> 00:12:02,283
What's that?

312
00:12:02,283 --> 00:12:03,413
(Satisfied)

313
00:12:05,183 --> 00:12:07,053
- It's a pork cutlet. - I see. A pork cutlet.

314
00:12:07,053 --> 00:12:10,323
Lunch always has to be a pork cutlet.

315
00:12:11,093 --> 00:12:12,423
Why wouldn't it be?

316
00:12:12,853 --> 00:12:14,423
(Chuckling)

317
00:12:16,093 --> 00:12:17,893
Why wouldn't lunch be a pork cutlet?

318
00:12:17,963 --> 00:12:19,493
(Chuckling)

319
00:12:20,263 --> 00:12:24,633
Were you traumatized when you were little?

320
00:12:24,773 --> 00:12:26,933
- Was I traumatized? - Why pork cutlets for traumas?

321
00:12:27,033 --> 00:12:28,543
If he was traumatized by it, he shouldn't eat it.

322
00:12:28,543 --> 00:12:29,943
- Traumas, not memories? - What?

323
00:12:30,003 --> 00:12:31,743
People usually ask if it's about childhood memories.

324
00:12:31,743 --> 00:12:34,043
- Who would call that a trauma? - He asked me a weird question.

325
00:12:34,213 --> 00:12:35,783
Here's what I meant. You could become obsessive...

326
00:12:35,783 --> 00:12:37,983
about a dish you couldn't eat when you were little.

327
00:12:38,353 --> 00:12:39,553
- That's what you meant? - Right.

328
00:12:39,553 --> 00:12:43,353
That's not it. Pork cutlets are men's comfort food.

329
00:12:43,583 --> 00:12:44,793
It's a simple dish.

330
00:12:45,123 --> 00:12:47,123
When I think about what to eat after waking up,

331
00:12:47,263 --> 00:12:49,093
that's the only dish that comes to my mind.

332
00:12:50,123 --> 00:12:51,263
It's a light meal too.

333
00:12:52,133 --> 00:12:54,263
(He's been looking forward to the first meal of his day.)

334
00:12:55,963 --> 00:12:57,603
(Today, it's a western-style pork cutlet.)

335
00:12:57,603 --> 00:12:59,473
- It's a collection of good food. - That looks good.

336
00:13:00,673 --> 00:13:03,143
(Placing it in his mouth)

337
00:13:03,143 --> 00:13:04,343
(Humming in approval)

338
00:13:05,713 --> 00:13:08,083
Yes. There are days when you crave that kind of pork cutlet.

339
00:13:08,083 --> 00:13:09,943
- Right. Not the crispy one. - The cafeteria-style pork cutlet.

340
00:13:09,943 --> 00:13:11,753
- Like the one from the cafeteria. - Yes.

341
00:13:12,213 --> 00:13:14,153
(Leaning forward)

342
00:13:14,153 --> 00:13:18,293
But isn't that too heavy for the first meal?

343
00:13:18,293 --> 00:13:19,653
Is it?

344
00:13:20,393 --> 00:13:22,563
- Yes. Isn't it heavy? - I...

345
00:13:22,563 --> 00:13:24,293
Having greasy food as the first meal...

346
00:13:24,293 --> 00:13:26,033
might upset the stomach.

347
00:13:26,533 --> 00:13:28,903
Whatever it is, it'll digest better than your food.

348
00:13:29,203 --> 00:13:30,973
- What do you usually eat? - It's better than your food.

349
00:13:30,973 --> 00:13:32,103
I eat frozen food.

350
00:13:33,203 --> 00:13:35,803
- I take food out from my freezer. - The leftover food.

351
00:13:36,173 --> 00:13:37,413
- It's leftover food. - I just microwave it.

352
00:13:37,413 --> 00:13:38,973
Your first meal was the hardest to watch.

353
00:13:40,173 --> 00:13:41,343
- I guess so. - You drank leftover coffee too.

354
00:13:41,883 --> 00:13:43,953
I guess pork cutlets are easier to digest than I thought.

355
00:13:45,153 --> 00:13:46,553
- You bet. Sometimes, - Come to think of it...

356
00:13:46,553 --> 00:13:48,223
- it's better than porridge. - Right.

357
00:13:48,223 --> 00:13:49,683
"Right." "Yes."

358
00:13:49,683 --> 00:13:51,153
(Pork cutlets are easily digestible.)

359
00:13:52,293 --> 00:13:54,063
(He's having his digestible meal in the dark.)

360
00:13:58,493 --> 00:14:00,933
(Yes, this is it.)

361
00:14:00,933 --> 00:14:03,973
Wait. You're eating your meal without watching TV?

362
00:14:04,503 --> 00:14:05,773
I'll watch something now while eating.

363
00:14:06,703 --> 00:14:08,343
- Hey. - Can you just watch for a bit?

364
00:14:08,673 --> 00:14:10,673
- You can't question his every move. - Okay.

365
00:14:11,273 --> 00:14:12,773
"Why did you close your eyes?"

366
00:14:12,773 --> 00:14:15,013
- "Why did you lean on the sofa?" - "Why did you eat?"

367
00:14:15,543 --> 00:14:17,013
I'll ask at intervals.

368
00:14:17,013 --> 00:14:18,283
- "Intervals." - "Intervals."

369
00:14:18,283 --> 00:14:19,483
- Yes. - My gosh.

370
00:14:20,753 --> 00:14:23,753
(He inhales the pasta after an interval of time.)

371
00:14:26,793 --> 00:14:28,093
You're eating with gusto.

372
00:14:28,923 --> 00:14:30,093
Why are you eating with gusto?

373
00:14:33,903 --> 00:14:35,603
Since when did you eat with gusto?

374
00:14:36,733 --> 00:14:38,273
Were you traumatized when you were little?

375
00:14:38,973 --> 00:14:41,003
Were you beaten up when you didn't eat with gusto?

376
00:14:41,943 --> 00:14:43,503
How could you eat with such gusto?

377
00:14:43,503 --> 00:14:44,543
(He's full of questions about Seong Jae.)

378
00:14:48,913 --> 00:14:51,253
(This is tasty.)

379
00:14:52,783 --> 00:14:54,853
(He eats...)

380
00:14:54,853 --> 00:14:56,383
(and continues to eat.)

381
00:14:58,153 --> 00:15:01,093
He must really like pork cutlets if he can eat them even after...

382
00:15:01,093 --> 00:15:02,963
- eating them several times. - It's true love.

383
00:15:05,663 --> 00:15:07,763
(Delighted)

384
00:15:09,003 --> 00:15:11,903
(That was satisfying for my first meal.)

385
00:15:13,003 --> 00:15:14,173
(He cleans up immediately.)

386
00:15:14,173 --> 00:15:17,873
(Sunny)

387
00:15:17,943 --> 00:15:19,813
It looks like a door to paradise.

388
00:15:22,513 --> 00:15:24,383
(Thinking)

389
00:15:29,983 --> 00:15:31,723
(He ponders for a moment...)

390
00:15:31,723 --> 00:15:33,993
(and heads somewhere.)

391
00:15:36,063 --> 00:15:37,763
(He brings out a pile of boxes.)

392
00:15:40,863 --> 00:15:42,833
(He plops down on the floor.)

393
00:15:44,773 --> 00:15:45,903
I wonder what that is.

394
00:15:45,903 --> 00:15:47,273
(What?)

395
00:15:48,603 --> 00:15:51,113
(A fire extinguisher)

396
00:15:51,443 --> 00:15:54,383
The renovation began when I was filming...

397
00:15:54,383 --> 00:15:55,983
my drama.

398
00:15:56,043 --> 00:15:59,483
I should have been here to watch the renovation progress.

399
00:15:59,483 --> 00:16:02,423
But I didn't have time to do that. So I couldn't.

400
00:16:02,423 --> 00:16:03,483
(Now that he has time, he'll decorate the house.)

401
00:16:03,483 --> 00:16:06,193
I've been living in that house for four months now.

402
00:16:06,353 --> 00:16:08,293
- That's the honeymoon phase. - Yes.

403
00:16:09,193 --> 00:16:11,733
I renovated the house and decorated it in black.

404
00:16:11,963 --> 00:16:13,363
I wanted to match...

405
00:16:13,363 --> 00:16:15,163
all of the other items in my house to black.

406
00:16:15,163 --> 00:16:16,903
- There are black ones? - Yes.

407
00:16:18,333 --> 00:16:20,303
He bought a black fire extinguisher.

408
00:16:23,143 --> 00:16:25,543
(What?)

409
00:16:32,253 --> 00:16:33,413
What color is this?

410
00:16:37,053 --> 00:16:39,223
(Putting it under the light)

411
00:16:40,863 --> 00:16:42,493
Gosh, I bought one in khaki.

412
00:16:42,623 --> 00:16:43,723
He did.

413
00:16:43,723 --> 00:16:46,663
But he's trying to match all of the items to black.

414
00:16:47,863 --> 00:16:49,633
(A disappointed sigh)

415
00:16:50,233 --> 00:16:52,633
(Trudging)

416
00:16:53,273 --> 00:16:54,803
Why did I buy one in khaki?

417
00:16:58,043 --> 00:16:59,043
(A khaki item is added to the interior design.)

418
00:16:59,043 --> 00:17:01,443
But having a khaki fire extinguisher looks pretty good there.

419
00:17:02,183 --> 00:17:04,753
(After getting ambushed by the khaki prop...)

420
00:17:06,413 --> 00:17:07,853
It's 3pm.

421
00:17:07,853 --> 00:17:09,883
(Let me see.)

422
00:17:15,723 --> 00:17:18,263
(He marches off somewhere.)

423
00:17:20,293 --> 00:17:21,303
(Flapping)

424
00:17:24,603 --> 00:17:26,573
(Shaking)

425
00:17:27,643 --> 00:17:30,203
With this, it's perfect, Wilson.

426
00:17:31,343 --> 00:17:32,873
(Pulling)

427
00:17:35,183 --> 00:17:36,613
(Black Wilson is born.)

428
00:17:36,613 --> 00:17:37,813
Wilson looks chic now.

429
00:17:39,053 --> 00:17:40,983
Doesn't it look good? Like a limited edition teddy bear?

430
00:17:40,983 --> 00:17:42,083
- Right. - Yes.

431
00:17:44,953 --> 00:17:46,693
(He picks up another box after recharging on black energy.)

432
00:17:48,093 --> 00:17:49,623
(Removing)

433
00:17:51,193 --> 00:17:53,093
(A black clock)

434
00:17:53,093 --> 00:17:54,633
- I bought a wall clock. - Gosh.

435
00:17:54,633 --> 00:17:55,733
- What is this? - Everything is black.

436
00:17:58,603 --> 00:18:00,133
Can you even tell time with that?

437
00:18:03,573 --> 00:18:05,713
(He strides into the dark.)

438
00:18:07,143 --> 00:18:09,343
(He places the black clock on the wall.)

439
00:18:09,343 --> 00:18:12,713
The wall was white, so it needed some black props.

440
00:18:15,523 --> 00:18:16,753
(Where should I hang it?)

441
00:18:16,753 --> 00:18:17,953
You're something else.

442
00:18:19,993 --> 00:18:21,323
I appreciate this kind of style.

443
00:18:21,323 --> 00:18:22,493
He's all about black.

444
00:18:25,093 --> 00:18:26,633
Gosh, I don't have the tools to put in a nail.

445
00:18:29,463 --> 00:18:31,803
(He puts a hold on the placement of the black clock for a moment.)

446
00:18:32,533 --> 00:18:33,833
I guess I'll make a trip then.

447
00:18:36,343 --> 00:18:38,113
(He uses a black electric shaver...)

448
00:18:38,113 --> 00:18:40,713
(and a black toothbrush to freshen up.)

449
00:18:41,383 --> 00:18:43,683
(This should do it.)

450
00:18:44,713 --> 00:18:47,453
(He gets dressed to go out.)

451
00:18:48,283 --> 00:18:50,423
(He's ready to face the world.)

452
00:18:50,993 --> 00:18:53,023
Once he goes out, the sunlight might hurt our eyes.

453
00:18:55,793 --> 00:18:59,663
(He strides outside the house.)

454
00:19:01,703 --> 00:19:04,273
(Rubbing his eyes)

455
00:19:05,473 --> 00:19:07,573
In vampire movies, when the sun comes up,

456
00:19:07,573 --> 00:19:09,873
- the vampires are in pain. - Their skin burns.

457
00:19:09,903 --> 00:19:11,613
I feel that way when I go outside these days.

458
00:19:11,613 --> 00:19:14,013
"Gosh, it's too bright." I can't look up at the sky.

459
00:19:14,013 --> 00:19:15,943
- Because your house is so dark. - You're surrounded by darkness.

460
00:19:16,813 --> 00:19:19,013
(Once he escapes from the dark house...)

461
00:19:19,013 --> 00:19:21,253
I left the house when the sun was high in the sky.

462
00:19:21,383 --> 00:19:24,823
It felt like the rainbow colors were unfolding before my eyes.

463
00:19:24,823 --> 00:19:25,853
When you leave your house,

464
00:19:25,853 --> 00:19:27,253
- it's so bright. - Right.

465
00:19:27,253 --> 00:19:29,163
(The outside world is indeed sunny.)

466
00:19:30,563 --> 00:19:32,063
This place is so popular.

467
00:19:32,593 --> 00:19:34,263
- Seongsu-dong? - Yes, I live in Seongsu-dong.

468
00:19:34,263 --> 00:19:35,303
That's my neighborhood.

469
00:19:35,463 --> 00:19:37,003
It's the trendiest and the most crowded place.

470
00:19:37,003 --> 00:19:38,473
- Gosh, totally. - Seongsu-dong. Yes.

471
00:19:38,633 --> 00:19:40,703
There are so many stores here now.

472
00:19:41,903 --> 00:19:43,373
(It's packed with people as it is a trendy spot.)

473
00:19:43,373 --> 00:19:44,473
It's so hot.

474
00:19:46,443 --> 00:19:48,013
(The temperature feels like 30°C, give or take.)

475
00:19:48,013 --> 00:19:50,443
(The weather suddenly gets hot.)

476
00:19:51,553 --> 00:19:52,653
It's so hot.

477
00:19:52,653 --> 00:19:54,353
I didn't know how hot it was outside.

478
00:19:54,353 --> 00:19:55,783
- So I dressed like that. - I didn't know it'd be this hot.

479
00:19:55,983 --> 00:19:57,623
It's really hot these days.

480
00:19:57,693 --> 00:19:58,993
I'm actually wearing...

481
00:19:58,993 --> 00:20:00,223
another long sleeve shirt underneath.

482
00:20:00,223 --> 00:20:01,293
- Gosh. - Oh, my.

483
00:20:01,293 --> 00:20:02,523
You might die from a heatstroke.

484
00:20:02,523 --> 00:20:03,623
I should've driven here.

485
00:20:05,263 --> 00:20:06,933
(Pulling out)

486
00:20:07,563 --> 00:20:08,663
(He's following the map to go somewhere.)

487
00:20:08,663 --> 00:20:10,203
It was my first time going there.

488
00:20:10,233 --> 00:20:12,273
(Since it's a hot day, he quickens his pace.)

489
00:20:13,533 --> 00:20:14,703
Gosh, there it is.

490
00:20:14,703 --> 00:20:15,873
(Happy to see the store)

491
00:20:16,943 --> 00:20:18,013
- What is it? - Finally.

492
00:20:18,013 --> 00:20:19,113
Where's he going?

493
00:20:20,490 --> 00:20:21,690
- What is it? - Finally.

494
00:20:21,690 --> 00:20:22,690
(He overcomes the heat and finally arrives.)

495
00:20:22,690 --> 00:20:23,890
"Outdoor."

496
00:20:23,890 --> 00:20:25,630
What is this store? I've never heard of it.

497
00:20:26,460 --> 00:20:28,070
Gosh, it's cool inside.

498
00:20:28,070 --> 00:20:29,200
(Even the TSM seems fascinated by it.)

499
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:30,470
It specializes in selling workwear.

500
00:20:31,270 --> 00:20:33,170
- I didn't know about this store. - That's a cute store.

501
00:20:36,870 --> 00:20:38,880
There are a lot of industrial shops nearby.

502
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:41,010
- Right. There are many factories. - That's known as the factory area.

503
00:20:41,010 --> 00:20:42,210
(Full of professional tools)

504
00:20:42,210 --> 00:20:45,280
I bought a black wall clock.

505
00:20:45,350 --> 00:20:47,950
(As it was a wall clock, he needed tools to put in a nail.)

506
00:20:47,950 --> 00:20:50,650
I went out to buy an electric drill, so I could put a hole in the wall.

507
00:20:50,650 --> 00:20:53,560
Since I own the house,

508
00:20:53,790 --> 00:20:55,230
I can drill holes.

509
00:20:55,230 --> 00:20:56,560
I was so excited about that.

510
00:20:57,900 --> 00:20:59,300
- Yes. - That's quite the thrill.

511
00:20:59,300 --> 00:21:00,800
- I love it. - It's your house.

512
00:21:01,270 --> 00:21:02,300
There it is.

513
00:21:03,300 --> 00:21:05,700
(He finds the electric drill he's been looking for.)

514
00:21:07,570 --> 00:21:09,140
(Thinking)

515
00:21:09,210 --> 00:21:11,910
Then are you going to buy that as a prop for your interior design?

516
00:21:12,610 --> 00:21:13,810
- The drill? - Yes.

517
00:21:13,810 --> 00:21:15,950
- That... - For his interior design?

518
00:21:16,150 --> 00:21:17,850
- Hey. - I thought you were trying...

519
00:21:17,850 --> 00:21:19,350
to display a cute drill on the wall.

520
00:21:19,350 --> 00:21:20,520
- He said he'd drill a hole. - Yes.

521
00:21:20,520 --> 00:21:22,190
Just now, he clearly said that he'd hang the clock.

522
00:21:22,190 --> 00:21:23,390
- Right. - He's asking a silly question...

523
00:21:23,390 --> 00:21:24,520
about drilling holes.

524
00:21:24,520 --> 00:21:25,890
I'll go home after five minutes.

525
00:21:26,790 --> 00:21:28,130
- You're staying here a long time. - Right.

526
00:21:28,130 --> 00:21:29,730
- I've been here too long. - You stayed longer than I thought.

527
00:21:30,830 --> 00:21:32,500
Excuse me.

528
00:21:33,500 --> 00:21:34,530
Hello.

529
00:21:34,530 --> 00:21:36,900
I just need to put a nail in the wall for a simple job.

530
00:21:36,900 --> 00:21:37,900
Which one would be best for it?

531
00:21:37,900 --> 00:21:40,070
That's the most popular product.

532
00:21:40,070 --> 00:21:41,170
- This one? - Yes.

533
00:21:41,610 --> 00:21:43,640
So I just need to make a hole in the fake wooden wall.

534
00:21:43,640 --> 00:21:45,510
- It won't need to be strong, right? - Right.

535
00:21:47,580 --> 00:21:49,510
I think this will do.

536
00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:50,880
This will be best to drill a hole...

537
00:21:50,880 --> 00:21:52,220
- in a fake wooden wall, right? - Yes.

538
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:54,180
I'll take this then.

539
00:21:54,180 --> 00:21:56,320
Having a drill will come in handy. You'll use it for so many things.

540
00:21:56,320 --> 00:21:57,960
- Okay. - It's your house, anyway.

541
00:22:01,590 --> 00:22:04,500
(Since he's at the store, he browses other products too.)

542
00:22:06,530 --> 00:22:08,070
(Grabbing)

543
00:22:10,030 --> 00:22:11,900
(Immediately responding to the color of his heart)

544
00:22:12,700 --> 00:22:14,370
What is that for?

545
00:22:14,370 --> 00:22:16,040
I bought it to wear at home.

546
00:22:16,040 --> 00:22:17,880
- What? At home? - You'll wear it at home?

547
00:22:17,880 --> 00:22:19,340
- At home? - You'll wear it at home?

548
00:22:19,510 --> 00:22:20,840
Wear it when your parents come over.

549
00:22:21,980 --> 00:22:24,010
Your mom might not be able to find you.

550
00:22:24,350 --> 00:22:26,850
"Seong Jae."

551
00:22:30,550 --> 00:22:31,860
- That's... - Oh, that.

552
00:22:31,920 --> 00:22:33,990
- It blocks out the noise. - I see.

553
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:36,060
- You're going to buy that? - Gosh.

554
00:22:36,330 --> 00:22:37,730
It was really cheap.

555
00:22:37,900 --> 00:22:38,930
It was only eight dollars.

556
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:41,530
I can't hear anything.

557
00:22:41,700 --> 00:22:43,270
(Another purchase)

558
00:22:43,270 --> 00:22:44,770
I should buy a pair.

559
00:22:45,570 --> 00:22:47,100
(Grabbing another item)

560
00:22:47,570 --> 00:22:48,910
What's that? What are the goggles for?

561
00:22:48,910 --> 00:22:50,740
- They are... - They must be protective goggles.

562
00:22:50,740 --> 00:22:52,980
- Yes. So nothing gets in the eyes. - I see.

563
00:22:53,080 --> 00:22:55,980
But do you actually need all of them?

564
00:22:57,280 --> 00:23:01,350
I don't buy things just based on needs.

565
00:23:04,190 --> 00:23:05,390
(Brimming with curiosity only about Seong Jae)

566
00:23:05,390 --> 00:23:07,260
Okay. Mr. Yuk.

567
00:23:07,260 --> 00:23:08,530
I'm sorry.

568
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:11,260
(A stuffed bag)

569
00:23:12,700 --> 00:23:14,160
(What do you think about my black goggles?)

570
00:23:14,160 --> 00:23:15,200
You put them on right away.

571
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:16,200
- Gosh, you look cool. - Yes.

572
00:23:16,330 --> 00:23:17,770
You look cool with anything.

573
00:23:17,770 --> 00:23:18,970
That's the Seongsu-dong style.

574
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:21,470
My gosh. You look pretty good with the goggles on.

575
00:23:21,470 --> 00:23:23,370
(He sets out while looking hip.)

576
00:23:27,310 --> 00:23:29,150
Do you often walk around alone?

577
00:23:29,180 --> 00:23:30,980
Yes. I often walk around.

578
00:23:30,980 --> 00:23:32,250
But I don't usually come out during the day.

579
00:23:32,250 --> 00:23:33,580
I see. Not during the day?

580
00:23:33,580 --> 00:23:35,220
- It must be uncomfortable. They... - I bet people go wild...

581
00:23:35,220 --> 00:23:37,050
- for him. - I like it when they recognize me.

582
00:23:37,050 --> 00:23:38,190
- Really? - Yes.

583
00:23:38,190 --> 00:23:40,320
We're quite similar in that regard.

584
00:23:41,360 --> 00:23:43,760
(I also enjoy being in the spotlight.)

585
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:45,500
- I like getting attention too. - Yes.

586
00:23:45,500 --> 00:23:47,000
- That's a rewarding feeling. - Right.

587
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:48,200
- Well... - That's how everyone feels.

588
00:23:48,900 --> 00:23:50,800
(Mr. Yuk walks home with the rewarding feeling as always.)

589
00:23:52,240 --> 00:23:54,470
(Staring)

590
00:23:55,170 --> 00:23:57,440
(He can't look away from something.)

591
00:23:58,010 --> 00:23:59,110
My gosh.

592
00:24:00,380 --> 00:24:01,450
This place is new.

593
00:24:01,450 --> 00:24:02,510
They're claw machines.

594
00:24:02,680 --> 00:24:04,250
- I mean... - This place is huge.

595
00:24:04,250 --> 00:24:05,420
- Gosh. - Oh, my. This place is huge.

596
00:24:05,420 --> 00:24:06,680
- What? - I love it here.

597
00:24:06,780 --> 00:24:08,890
When I see a claw machine arcade, I go in.

598
00:24:09,250 --> 00:24:10,850
That's just how I am.

599
00:24:10,950 --> 00:24:12,390
"Why can't I win this?"

600
00:24:12,490 --> 00:24:15,130
So there's this mind game between the claw machine...

601
00:24:15,130 --> 00:24:16,590
and the player, you know?

602
00:24:16,830 --> 00:24:19,360
I can't just walk past the arcade without going in.

603
00:24:20,200 --> 00:24:21,700
Which one should I go for?

604
00:24:22,830 --> 00:24:25,270
I don't think there aren't any black dolls.

605
00:24:25,970 --> 00:24:27,340
(Staring)

606
00:24:27,340 --> 00:24:28,770
I see a black doll over there.

607
00:24:32,110 --> 00:24:33,680
I'll go for Toothless.

608
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:36,180
- There are black dolls. - There really are black dolls.

609
00:24:36,950 --> 00:24:38,820
All right. Five coins.

610
00:24:38,820 --> 00:24:40,020
(They're equivalent to 5 dollars.)

611
00:24:40,020 --> 00:24:41,620
Yes, you need to charge five dollars.

612
00:24:41,620 --> 00:24:43,490
He'll need to spend at least 50 dollars by default.

613
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:46,560
- Are you good at this? - I'm good at claw machines.

614
00:24:46,660 --> 00:24:47,920
- Really? - Gosh.

615
00:24:48,630 --> 00:24:50,090
(Looking proud)

616
00:24:50,990 --> 00:24:53,130
(Let me see.)

617
00:24:53,130 --> 00:24:54,230
I think...

618
00:24:55,200 --> 00:24:56,430
this guy here...

619
00:24:56,430 --> 00:24:57,430
(Let me start with him.)

620
00:24:58,770 --> 00:25:00,970
(He calmly scans the dolls...)

621
00:25:00,970 --> 00:25:02,610
(while his hand carefully controls the joystick.)

622
00:25:02,740 --> 00:25:04,810
I'm really confident about this.

623
00:25:05,010 --> 00:25:06,140
I'm really good.

624
00:25:06,140 --> 00:25:07,340
Here's a useful tip.

625
00:25:07,340 --> 00:25:08,810
Just because the head is heavy,

626
00:25:08,810 --> 00:25:10,510
you can't try to grab the heavy part.

627
00:25:10,580 --> 00:25:12,480
This game isn't about grabbing the doll...

628
00:25:12,480 --> 00:25:13,880
and dropping it off.

629
00:25:13,980 --> 00:25:15,850
It's about dragging the doll.

630
00:25:15,850 --> 00:25:16,890
- Right. - I see.

631
00:25:16,890 --> 00:25:18,890
For big ones, you have to move them first.

632
00:25:18,960 --> 00:25:20,860
I think I can win a doll only with three dollars.

633
00:25:20,860 --> 00:25:22,990
I don't expect to get anything out of the first dollar.

634
00:25:22,990 --> 00:25:24,930
I'll just try to see how heavy the doll is.

635
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:26,700
Then with the second dollar,

636
00:25:26,700 --> 00:25:29,500
I just need to get the tail over the prize chute.

637
00:25:29,500 --> 00:25:31,740
With the third dollar, as long as I have the tail over the chute,

638
00:25:31,740 --> 00:25:34,640
I can pull the doll. Then it will fall into the chute.

639
00:25:34,770 --> 00:25:36,110
It sounds like he plays this game a lot.

640
00:25:36,170 --> 00:25:38,040
It's a piece of cake. So easy.

641
00:25:38,040 --> 00:25:39,080
He sounds like an expert.

642
00:25:39,080 --> 00:25:41,040
Experts have a plan before they play like him.

643
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:44,250
I recently met someone who spent 100,000 dollars on claw machines.

644
00:25:44,650 --> 00:25:46,920
- Really? - He was the king of claw machines.

645
00:25:46,920 --> 00:25:48,650
So how is he doing these days?

646
00:25:48,650 --> 00:25:50,820
Well, he's struggling for sure.

647
00:25:51,420 --> 00:25:54,360
Listen. I actually saw him play in person.

648
00:25:54,490 --> 00:25:55,930
He didn't try to win it on his first try.

649
00:25:55,930 --> 00:25:56,960
He came up with a plan...

650
00:25:56,960 --> 00:25:57,960
- for 10 tries. - He spent 100,000 dollars?

651
00:25:57,960 --> 00:25:59,600
Then he built a tower and won the doll.

652
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:00,830
- Right. - Yes.

653
00:26:01,230 --> 00:26:02,470
(Another king of claw machines starts playing.)

654
00:26:02,470 --> 00:26:03,530
- Let's see. - But...

655
00:26:03,530 --> 00:26:05,100
- Let's see how good he is. - Yes, let's watch.

656
00:26:06,970 --> 00:26:08,440
(He carefully presses the button.)

657
00:26:09,670 --> 00:26:11,410
Well, his plan sounds perfect so far.

658
00:26:14,410 --> 00:26:16,250
- That's how I need to drag it. - Right, you have to drag it.

659
00:26:16,250 --> 00:26:17,880
You have to turn it like this.

660
00:26:18,010 --> 00:26:19,980
- It should go that way. - He wasted a dollar.

661
00:26:19,980 --> 00:26:21,180
He said he'd use the first dollar...

662
00:26:21,180 --> 00:26:22,390
- on checking the weight. - Yes.

663
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:24,920
(Careful)

664
00:26:25,790 --> 00:26:27,190
(Pressing)

665
00:26:27,590 --> 00:26:28,860
This went in well.

666
00:26:29,890 --> 00:26:31,090
This went in well.

667
00:26:32,100 --> 00:26:33,100
My gosh. What?

668
00:26:33,100 --> 00:26:34,830
- I knew it. It can't work. - It's gotten farther.

669
00:26:34,830 --> 00:26:35,970
It's gotten farther now.

670
00:26:36,900 --> 00:26:38,740
I should flip this one then.

671
00:26:38,740 --> 00:26:39,740
(He changes his target to the doll near the chute.)

672
00:26:43,010 --> 00:26:45,640
- That's it. Flip over. - There you go.

673
00:26:49,010 --> 00:26:50,980
- That's it. - You got it. You're close.

674
00:26:50,980 --> 00:26:53,180
(Surprised)

675
00:26:54,450 --> 00:26:57,050
(Just like his strategy, the tail is over the chute.)

676
00:26:57,320 --> 00:26:58,860
I should drag it by the legs.

677
00:26:59,520 --> 00:27:00,890
I'll hook it.

678
00:27:05,500 --> 00:27:06,960
(Falling)

679
00:27:07,260 --> 00:27:10,800
(Disappointed)

680
00:27:12,400 --> 00:27:15,010
- I can win with three more dollars. - Please insert the card.

681
00:27:16,870 --> 00:27:19,110
(Determined)

682
00:27:19,940 --> 00:27:21,950
(This time around, he boldly goes for the head.)

683
00:27:21,950 --> 00:27:23,110
- He said not to go after the head. - That's it.

684
00:27:23,110 --> 00:27:24,310
- My goodness. He's close. - He got it.

685
00:27:24,310 --> 00:27:26,020
(Disappointed)

686
00:27:30,490 --> 00:27:32,520
(Gosh, this is so annoying!)

687
00:27:32,520 --> 00:27:33,520
Darn it.

688
00:27:33,590 --> 00:27:35,360
Can't I just hit this?

689
00:27:35,360 --> 00:27:37,890
(Getting spiteful)

690
00:27:42,130 --> 00:27:43,800
- Right. Pick up the head... - Pick up the head.

691
00:27:43,800 --> 00:27:45,070
and flip it backward.

692
00:27:45,070 --> 00:27:46,440
Gosh.

693
00:27:46,540 --> 00:27:48,310
I lost my patience at that point.

694
00:27:48,310 --> 00:27:49,310
You have to keep calm.
(Getting mad)

695
00:27:51,470 --> 00:27:53,440
Let's try 1 more time. I'll spend another 3 dollars.

696
00:27:53,510 --> 00:27:54,880
Please insert your card.

697
00:27:54,880 --> 00:27:56,050
He's spending over 10 dollars now.

698
00:27:56,380 --> 00:27:58,050
Hit it like this.

699
00:27:58,050 --> 00:27:59,250
(I'll make it happen!)

700
00:27:59,250 --> 00:28:00,380
When it goes back...

701
00:28:00,920 --> 00:28:02,890
(Here comes my super move!)

702
00:28:02,890 --> 00:28:03,950
When it goes back...

703
00:28:04,090 --> 00:28:06,190
It can't move at that point.

704
00:28:06,960 --> 00:28:09,090
(Missing)

705
00:28:09,460 --> 00:28:10,690
(What?)

706
00:28:11,290 --> 00:28:13,600
- I lost my patience. - You've lost your patience.

707
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:15,300
But since you're handsome,

708
00:28:15,300 --> 00:28:16,900
it will be fun to play even if you don't get anything.

709
00:28:17,230 --> 00:28:18,540
- What are you saying? - What are you saying?

710
00:28:18,570 --> 00:28:20,570
- What are you saying? - What?

711
00:28:20,570 --> 00:28:22,610
It looks like he's having fun since he's handsome.

712
00:28:22,610 --> 00:28:24,310
- The whole situation? - Right.

713
00:28:24,610 --> 00:28:26,540
(He focuses with his exciting face.)

714
00:28:28,140 --> 00:28:30,380
Please!

715
00:28:32,480 --> 00:28:34,150
He's really hooked on that.

716
00:28:35,620 --> 00:28:37,690
- Another three dollars. - Please insert your card.

717
00:28:37,990 --> 00:28:39,190
This is really the last game.

718
00:28:39,690 --> 00:28:41,360
Right. Move it to one side.

719
00:28:41,760 --> 00:28:43,030
There you go.

720
00:28:44,590 --> 00:28:46,730
- Let's get it this time. - Gosh.

721
00:28:48,100 --> 00:28:51,330
(Please give me the stuffed toy.)

722
00:28:51,330 --> 00:28:53,470
- No! - Gosh.

723
00:28:53,470 --> 00:28:55,540
- It's working. - It's working.

724
00:28:55,540 --> 00:28:56,670
Gosh!

725
00:28:59,780 --> 00:29:00,810
He got it.

726
00:29:01,750 --> 00:29:03,550
(Yes!)

727
00:29:04,610 --> 00:29:08,080
(He finally succeeds!)

728
00:29:08,080 --> 00:29:09,150
He finally did it.

729
00:29:11,150 --> 00:29:15,760
(He's about to fly away with joy.)

730
00:29:16,930 --> 00:29:17,930
Gosh, this is amazing.

731
00:29:19,130 --> 00:29:20,500
(A great achievement made with 18 dollars!)

732
00:29:20,500 --> 00:29:21,500
Amazing.

733
00:29:23,070 --> 00:29:24,300
(He won the black stuffed toy with his tenacity.)

734
00:29:24,300 --> 00:29:25,300
Goodbye.

735
00:29:28,310 --> 00:29:29,940
(I had a great time.)

736
00:29:29,940 --> 00:29:31,440
Gosh, you look excited.

737
00:29:33,180 --> 00:29:34,510
It felt great to come out in the morning.

738
00:29:34,510 --> 00:29:36,980
(He keeps smiling during his successful outing in the day.)

739
00:29:38,250 --> 00:29:39,620
- He's returning to his cave now? - Yes.

740
00:29:39,620 --> 00:29:40,750
Don't go in!

741
00:29:42,250 --> 00:29:44,750
(He returns to his cave.)

742
00:29:46,160 --> 00:29:47,320
Why is it so dark?

743
00:29:48,290 --> 00:29:49,760
- It was nice being outside. - Gosh.

744
00:29:50,090 --> 00:29:51,630
Gosh, it's dark.

745
00:29:53,100 --> 00:29:56,130
(He takes out something in the dark.)

746
00:29:57,730 --> 00:29:58,940
It's that vest from earlier.

747
00:30:00,700 --> 00:30:02,740
(He puts on his glow-in-the-dark vest.)

748
00:30:03,470 --> 00:30:06,040
(He wears safety goggles...)

749
00:30:06,040 --> 00:30:07,480
(and work gloves.)

750
00:30:08,680 --> 00:30:10,210
(He's ready to make holes with his drill prepared as well.)

751
00:30:11,680 --> 00:30:13,750
(The Black House finally becomes bright.)

752
00:30:13,750 --> 00:30:14,750
The lights are finally on.

753
00:30:16,090 --> 00:30:17,150
He even has those?

754
00:30:17,150 --> 00:30:18,690
(He makes the finishing touch with the headset.)

755
00:30:18,690 --> 00:30:19,790
I can drill holes.

756
00:30:19,790 --> 00:30:21,490
(Excited about making a hole for the first time)

757
00:30:21,790 --> 00:30:23,790
That feels like you're going against the rules.

758
00:30:24,490 --> 00:30:25,900
Drilling holes in your house.

759
00:30:27,430 --> 00:30:29,770
(He chooses the position of the hole...)

760
00:30:29,770 --> 00:30:31,170
(and doesn't hesitate.)

761
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:33,070
(Drilling)

762
00:30:33,400 --> 00:30:34,400
(Groaning)

763
00:30:34,400 --> 00:30:36,170
- What's wrong? - It isn't going in.

764
00:30:36,370 --> 00:30:37,470
Why won't it go in?

765
00:30:38,810 --> 00:30:40,140
Is it a concrete wall?

766
00:30:41,380 --> 00:30:42,380
This is a plaster wall!

767
00:30:43,610 --> 00:30:45,650
(I definitely thought it was a wooden wall.)

768
00:30:46,280 --> 00:30:48,020
You said it was a wooden wall earlier!

769
00:30:48,250 --> 00:30:50,090
It felt like wood when I hit against it.

770
00:30:51,090 --> 00:30:52,490
You hit against it to find out? It's not even a watermelon.

771
00:30:52,590 --> 00:30:53,620
My gosh.

772
00:30:54,960 --> 00:30:56,060
How frustrating.

773
00:30:56,330 --> 00:30:57,490
I think it's a plaster wall.

774
00:30:57,490 --> 00:30:58,500
(Looking closely)

775
00:30:58,500 --> 00:31:00,230
Gosh, I can't see anything.

776
00:31:00,400 --> 00:31:01,500
How do I turn on the lights?

777
00:31:01,730 --> 00:31:03,430
He doesn't even call for Google as he's in a hurry.

778
00:31:03,870 --> 00:31:04,930
Right.

779
00:31:04,930 --> 00:31:06,900
(He even uses his flashlight of his phone.)

780
00:31:08,140 --> 00:31:09,910
- Gosh, this is a plaster wall. - It's a plaster wall?

781
00:31:10,340 --> 00:31:12,240
